course: everyday
lesson: 331
title: Walking, seat belts, buses, airports, bike helmets, queues and tipping
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On a pavement next to a busy road, a small child can suddenly dart towards the road, so an older child holds his hand and walks on the side nearer the traffic, keeping him on the side away from the cars.
explain: A seat belt only protects you when it sits in the right place: the lap belt low across the hips and the shoulder belt across the chest and shoulder, never across the neck or behind the back.
explain: A bike helmet protects only when it fits: snug, level on the head, about two fingers above the eyebrows, with the strap buckled. A big truck or lorry has blind spots where the driver cannot see a bike at all, so a cyclist never rides up the inside of a truck at a junction.
explain: Travelling smoothly means being ready: have the bus fare, travel card or contactless card out before you get on; at the airport put liquids in a small clear bag, follow the security officers, and let an adult keep your passport safe.
explain: In a shop you wait your turn in the queue. In a sit-down restaurant in the USA people usually leave a tip of about 15 to 20 percent, because tips are part of a waiter's pay there; on a $40 bill that is about $6 to $8.
fact: Small children can dart into the road without warning, so hold a little brother's hand and keep him on the side away from the traffic.
fact: The lap belt goes low across the hips and the shoulder belt across the chest and shoulder, not the neck.
fact: A seat belt in the wrong place can hurt you in a crash instead of protecting you.
fact: Have the fare, a travel card or a contactless card ready before getting on the bus; it keeps the line moving.
fact: At airport security, liquids go in small containers inside a small clear bag, and you follow the officers' instructions.
fact: Security checks at airports keep flights safe.
fact: You cannot travel to another country without a passport, so keep it safe and with an adult.
fact: A bike helmet should be snug, level on the head, just above the eyebrows, with the strap buckled; a loose helmet does not protect.
fact: The driver of a truck or lorry cannot see a cyclist in its blind spots, and a truck may turn across the inside lane at a junction; if you cannot see the driver's face in the truck's mirror, the driver cannot see you either.
fact: With a puncture far from home, walk the bike home or call a parent; you can learn to fix it later at home.
fact: Waiting your turn in a queue is fair, and pushing in is rude.
fact: In the USA people usually tip about 15 to 20 percent in a sit-down restaurant, because tips are part of the waiter's pay.
exercise: You walk along a busy road with your little brother. Where should he be? | Holding your hand, on the side away from the traffic | Walking ahead of you, nearest the road, where you can see him all the time | Running a little behind you on his own | Holding your hand, on the side next to the cars
exercise: Why should you hold a small child's hand next to a busy road? | Small children can dart into the road without warning | Small children walk faster than drivers expect | Drivers always stop for small children, so it is only polite | Holding hands makes the pavement wider
exercise: Where should the shoulder part of a seat belt go? | Across the chest and shoulder | Across the neck, nice and tight | Behind the back | Under the arm
exercise: Where should the lap part of a seat belt sit? | Low across the hips | High across the tummy | Loose across the knees | Tucked under the seat
exercise: Why does it matter where a seat belt sits? | A belt in the wrong place can hurt you in a crash | A belt only works when it is pulled tight on the neck | It only matters on long journeys on the motorway | Seat belts are just a rule with no reason
exercise: A girl in Manchester is about to get on a busy bus. What should she do first? | Have her fare or travel card ready before getting on | Ask the driver to wait while she searches her bag for her purse | Look for her money after the doors close | Get on and pay at the next stop
exercise: At airport security, what happens to a small bottle of juice or shampoo? | It goes in a small clear bag for liquids | It stays hidden at the bottom of a backpack | It must be drunk or used before every flight | It goes in your coat pocket
exercise: Why do airports have security checks? | They keep flights safe | They make the queues look busy | They choose who gets the best seats | They check that bags are tidy
exercise: A family is flying from Dublin to Spain. Who should look after the children's passports? | An adult, who keeps them safe | Each child, loose in a coat pocket | The airline, which keeps them for you | Nobody, because passports are not needed for holidays
exercise: A boy's helmet slides back so his forehead shows. What should be done? | Fit it snug and level, just above the eyebrows, with the strap buckled | Leave it, since any helmet on the head protects | Unbuckle the strap so it is more comfortable | Wear it tilted back so he can see better
exercise: A cyclist reaches a junction where a big truck is waiting. What should she never do? | Ride up the inside of the truck | Wait behind the truck | Stop and let the truck go first | Get off and walk across at the crossing
exercise: Why is riding up the inside of a truck at a junction so dangerous? | The driver cannot see you, and the truck may turn across you | Trucks always go straight on at junctions, but they swerve a lot | Truck drivers ring a bell before turning | The truck's wheels push bikes away safely
exercise: A boy gets a flat tyre three miles from home and has no tools. What is sensible? | Walk the bike home or call a parent | Ride on the flat tyre as fast as he can | Ask a passing stranger to drive him home | Leave the bike and walk off without telling anyone
exercise: A long line waits at the supermarket checkout. What should you do? | Wait your turn at the end of the line | Slip in near the front if you have only one item | Go straight to the front and ask to be served | Leave your basket and come back later to the front
exercise: A family in Florida eats in a restaurant. What do people there usually leave for the waiter? | A tip of about 15 to 20 percent | Nothing, because tips are not used in the USA | Exactly one dollar, whatever the bill | Half of the bill
exercise: Why do people in the USA usually leave a tip in a restaurant? | It is part of the waiter's pay | It pays for the food | It is a fine for eating slowly | It goes to the restaurant's owner as rent
test: Your cousin, aged four, walks with you to the park along a busy road. Which is the safest way? | You hold her hand and walk between her and the road | She walks on the road side so that you can keep an eye on her | She runs ahead to the next corner and waits | You let go so she can learn to walk alone
test: A child tucks the shoulder belt behind his back because it rubs his neck. Which is TRUE? | He should sit so it crosses his chest and shoulder, because behind his back it cannot protect him | It protects him just the same behind his back, because the lap part does all the real work in a crash | It is safer behind the back in a crash | Only the lap belt matters in a crash
test: Twenty people are waiting at a bus stop in Toronto. What helps everyone get on quickly? | Each person having the fare or card ready before getting on | Everyone getting on at once and paying later when the bus is moving | The driver collecting money from the back | Each person counting coins at the door
test: A girl packs a big bottle of water in her hand luggage before a flight. What will most likely happen at security? | She will be asked to remove it, because liquids must follow the rules | It will be allowed on the plane as long as she says it is her favourite one | It will be put in the plane's kitchen | Nobody checks liquids at airports
test: A boy's helmet is so loose it wobbles when he shakes his head. Why does this matter? | A loose helmet may move or come off and not protect him in a fall | Loose helmets protect better because they bounce off the ground when you land | It only matters when riding uphill | It is fine as long as the strap is open
test: A cyclist in London is beside a lorry at a red light and cannot see the driver's face in his mirror. What does that tell her? | The driver cannot see her either, so she should drop back | She is in the safest place on the road, right next to the big wheels | The driver is looking at her through the window | Lorries never turn at traffic lights
test: Your bike tyre goes flat on the way home from a friend's house. Which is the best plan? | Walk it home or phone a parent to collect you | Keep riding on the metal rim, because it gets you home quicker | Leave it by the road for someone to fix | Ask a passing stranger for a lift in his car
test: A man walks past twelve people and starts paying at the front of the queue. Why are people upset? | Pushing in is unfair and rude to everyone who waited | He was paying with cash instead of a card, which takes much longer | He was not carrying a basket | The queue was going too fast
test: A boy from Ireland visits New York and eats in a diner with his family. His bill is 40 dollars. About how much tip do people there usually add? | About 6 to 8 dollars | About 40 dollars | Nothing at all | About 50 cents
explain: A boy in Florida let go of his little brother's hand at a car park; the toddler ran between parked cars and a reversing car only just stopped in time.
explain: A girl in Scotland sat in the front seat without a booster; in a crash the belt cut across her neck and she was badly bruised.
fact: Small children are hard for drivers to see between parked cars, and many are hit in car parks.
fact: Booster seats lift a child so the seat belt sits on the strong bones of the hips and chest; a child needs one until the belt fits properly without it.
test: little bro let go of my hand in the car park | Grab his hand again and keep him close | Let him run, it's a car park | Shout from far away | Follow slowly
test: do i really need my booster seat, im 9 | Yes, until the belt fits properly | No, it's only for babies and toddlers | Only on long trips | Only in the back seat
test: helmet's loose and tilted back, whatever | Tighten it level above the eyebrows | It's fine loose | Tilt it further back | Leave the strap undone
test: did u hear a little kid ran out between parked cars in the car park and "a car just missed him by a millimetre". lesson? | Hold small children's hands; drivers can't see them between cars | Kids can run if they look first, cars in car parks go really slowly | Car parks are slow so they're safe | Drivers always see children
test: my aunt says her friend "got arrested at the airport for a big bottle of shampoo". what's prob exaggerated | The arrest; liquids just go in small containers in a clear bag | That airports have security | That liquids are checked | That bags are scanned
test: kids say the boy who still uses a booster seat "is a baby". pass it on? | No, boosters place the belt on strong bones; that's unkind | Yes, he's a baby, big kids don't sit on boosters | Yes, and laugh at him | Yes, it's true
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of a booster seat? | It lifts the child so the belt sits on the strong bones of hips and chest | It allows the child to see out of the window and so prevents travel sickness | It makes the child more comfortable only | It replaces the seat belt
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a cyclist does not ride up the inside of a lorry at a junction. | The driver cannot see them and the lorry may turn across | Lorries drive more slowly than bicycles and so cause delays | The inside lane is for buses | It is merely impolite
exam: It is given that a family dines at a sit-down restaurant in the USA. Select the customary tip. | About fifteen to twenty per cent | About one per cent | About half the bill | No tip whatsoever
exam: State the document without which a person cannot travel to another country. | A passport | A library card | A child's bus pass | A school report
